\title{Join-Saturation for Matchings with Isolated Vertices:\\
Foundations and the Cameron--Puleo Recurrence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 26, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, from first principles, the basic theory of graph \emph{saturation numbers}
underlying the Cameron--Puleo and K\'aszonyi--Tuza circle of results, and we situate within it
a specific extremal recurrence for cones over matchings with isolated vertices. For a forbidden
graph $H$, a host $G$ is \emph{$H$-saturated} when it is $H$-free yet adding any missing edge
creates a copy of $H$; the \emph{saturation number} $\sat(n,H)$ is the minimum edge count over
$H$-saturated graphs on $n$ vertices, while the \emph{extremal number} $\ex(n,H)$ is the
maximum edge count over $H$-free graphs. We establish three foundational results: (i) an
$H$-saturated graph exists whenever $H$ has an edge, so $\sat$ is well defined; (ii) the bound
$\sat(n,H) \le \ex(n,H)$; and (iii) the exact apex-join identity $e(K_1 \vee H) = |\Vrm(H)| +
e(H)$, the structural origin of the linear term in the recurrence. We then define precisely the
family $F = tK_2 \cup qK_1$ and state the recurrence $\sat(n, K_1 \vee (tK_2 \cup qK_1)) = (n-1)
+ \sat(n-1, tK_2 \cup qK_1)$ for $t \ge 1$, $q \ge 1$, $n > 2t+q$. The upper bound follows from
the cone identity; the lower bound is known for $t = 1, 2$ (Cameron--Puleo) and open in
general. We give proof sketches, an algorithmic toolkit, applications, and a program for the
general case by induction on the matching size.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Extremal graph theory studies how global numerical constraints force or forbid local structure.
The classical object is the \emph{Tur\'an (extremal) number} $\ex(n,H)$, the maximum number of
edges in an $n$-vertex graph containing no copy of $H$. Its dual is the \emph{saturation
number} $\sat(n,H)$, the minimum number of edges in an $n$-vertex graph that is $H$-free but
maximally so: adding any nonedge creates a copy of $H$. Both record $H$-free graphs that cannot
be enlarged within the $H$-free world---$\ex$ the richest, $\sat$ the poorest. For most $H$ of
interest $\ex(n,H) = \Theta(n^2)$ while $\sat(n,H) = \Theta(n)$ (K\'aszonyi--Tuza).

A recent theme concerns saturation numbers of \emph{cones} (apex joins): $K_1 \vee H$ adds a
single apex vertex adjacent to all of $H$. Cameron and Puleo proved recurrences expressing
$\sat(n, K_1 \vee F)$ via $\sat(n-1, F)$. The cleanest concerns $F = tK_2 \cup qK_1$, a
matching of size $t$ with $q$ isolated vertices: $\sat(n, K_1 \vee F) = (n-1) + \sat(n-1, F)$,
proved for $t = 1, 2$ and conjectured for all $t$.

This paper makes the foundations rigorous---existence of saturated graphs, the
$\sat \le \ex$ bridge, the cone identity---and states the recurrence and its base family
precisely, isolating the open inequality and a program for the general case. All graphs are
finite and simple; $e(G)$ is the edge count, $H \sqsubseteq G$ means $H$ embeds in $G$,
$\bot$ is the empty graph, $G \sqcup \{ab\}$ adds the edge $ab$, $K_1 \vee H$ is the cone,
$tK_2$ a matching, $qK_1$ isolated vertices, $K_{r+1}$ the complete graph.

\section{Definitions}

We work over a finite vertex type; the canonical host is $\Fin\,n = \{0, \dots, n-1\}$.

\begin{definition}[Edge count]
For a graph $G$ on a finite vertex set, $\ecount(G) = |E(G)|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$H$-saturation]
Let $H$ be a graph on $W$ and $G$ a graph on $V$. Then $G$ is \emph{$H$-saturated} if:
(1) $H$ does not embed into $G$; and (2) for all distinct $a,b \in V$ with $ab \notin E(G)$,
$H \sqsubseteq G \sqcup \{ab\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Extremal number]
$\ex(n,H) = \max\{\ecount(G) : G \text{ on } \Fin\,n,\ H \text{ free over } G\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Saturation number]
$\sat(n,H) = \min\{\ecount(G) : G \text{ on } \Fin\,n,\ G \text{ is } H\text{-saturated}\}$,
with the value $0$ by convention if no $H$-saturated graph exists (Theorem~\ref{thm:exist}
rules this out when $H$ has an edge).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cone / apex join]
For $H$ on $V$, the \emph{cone} $K_1 \vee H$ is the graph on $V \cup \{\ast\}$ with
$\ast \sim v$ for all $v \in V$, $u \sim v \iff u \sim_H v$ for $u, v \in V$, and
$\ast \not\sim \ast$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Matching with isolated vertices]
\label{def:family}
For $t, q \ge 0$, the graph $F = tK_2 \cup qK_1$ lives on $\Fin(2t+q)$, with $i \sim j$ iff
$i < 2t$, $j < 2t$, $\lfloor i/2 \rfloor = \lfloor j/2 \rfloor$, and $i \ne j$. Vertices $2k,
2k+1$ ($k < t$) form the $k$-th matching edge; vertices of index $\ge 2t$ are isolated.
\end{definition}

\section{Foundational lemmas}

\begin{lemma}[No edge embeds into the empty graph]
\label{lem:freebot}
If $H$ has an edge $ab$, then $H$ is free over $\bot$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
An embedding $f : H \hookrightarrow \bot$ would map the edge $ab$ to an edge $f(a)f(b)$ of
$\bot$; but $\bot$ has no edges.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Adding a new edge strictly increases the count]
\label{lem:addedge}
If $G$ is finite, $a \ne b$, and $ab \notin E(G)$, then $\ecount(G) < \ecount(G \sqcup \{ab\})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$E(G \sqcup \{ab\}) = E(G) \cup \{ab\}$ is a strict superset of $E(G)$, since $ab \notin E(G)$
and $a \ne b$ makes $ab$ a genuine non-loop edge. Strict monotonicity of cardinality applies.
\end{proof}

\section{Existence of saturated graphs}

\begin{theorem}[Maximal-free is saturated; existence]
\label{thm:exist}
If $H$ has at least one edge, then for every $n$ there is an $H$-saturated graph on $\Fin\,n$;
hence $\sat(n,H)$ is well defined.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The set of $H$-free graphs on $\Fin\,n$ is finite and nonempty ($\bot$ is $H$-free by
Lemma~\ref{lem:freebot}), so there is an $H$-free graph $G$ of maximum edge count. If some
nonedge $ab$ satisfied $H \not\sqsubseteq G \sqcup \{ab\}$, then $G \sqcup \{ab\}$ would be
$H$-free with strictly more edges (Lemma~\ref{lem:addedge}), contradicting maximality. So every
nonedge addition creates $H$, and $G$ is $H$-saturated.
\end{proof}

\section{The classical bound $\sat \le \ex$}

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:satex}
If $H$ has at least one edge, then $\sat(n,H) \le \ex(n,H)$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:exist} there is an $H$-saturated $G_0$, so $\sat(n,H) \le \ecount(G_0)$.
Since $G_0$ is $H$-free, $\ecount(G_0) \le \ex(n,H)$. Chain the two inequalities.
\end{proof}

\section{The apex join and the linear term}

\begin{theorem}[Cone edge identity]
\label{thm:cone}
For a graph $H$ on a finite vertex set $V$,
\[
\ecount(K_1 \vee H) = |V| + \ecount(H).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The edges of $K_1 \vee H$ split disjointly into the $|V|$ apex edges $\{\ast, v\}$ ($v \in V$),
in bijection with $V$, and the internal edges, in edge-count-preserving bijection with $E(H)$
under $V \hookrightarrow V \cup \{\ast\}$. The two classes are disjoint (an apex edge contains
$\ast$, an internal edge does not) and exhaust $E(K_1 \vee H)$; both maps are injective, so
cardinalities add.
\end{proof}

\section{The Cameron--Puleo recurrence for cones of matchings}

Recall $F = tK_2 \cup qK_1$ from Definition~\ref{def:family}.

\begin{conjecture}[Cameron--Puleo recurrence; proved for $t=1,2$]
\label{conj:main}
For every $t \ge 1$, $q \ge 1$, and $n > 2t+q$,
\begin{equation}
\sat\!\bigl(n, K_1 \vee (tK_2 \cup qK_1)\bigr) = (n-1) + \sat\!\bigl(n-1, tK_2 \cup qK_1\bigr).
\tag{$\star$}
\end{equation}
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{Upper bound (from Theorem~\ref{thm:cone})}
Let $G'$ be a minimum $F$-saturated graph on $\Fin(n-1)$ (it exists by Theorem~\ref{thm:exist},
as $F$ has an edge when $t \ge 1$). The cone $K_1 \vee G'$ is $(K_1 \vee F)$-saturated, and by
Theorem~\ref{thm:cone},
\[
e(K_1 \vee G') = (n-1) + e(G') = (n-1) + \sat(n-1, F),
\]
giving the $\le$ direction of $(\star)$.

\subsection{Lower bound (open crux)}
The $\ge$ direction is equivalent to a single inequality.

\begin{conjecture}[Join-lowering inequality]
\label{conj:lower}
For any graph $F$ with at least one edge and $n > |\Vrm(F)| + 1$,
\begin{equation}
\sat(n, K_1 \vee F) \ge (n-1) + \sat(n-1, F).
\tag{$\dagger$}
\end{equation}
\end{conjecture}

Combining $(\dagger)$ with the upper bound yields $(\star)$. Heuristically, a minimum
$(K_1 \vee F)$-saturated graph contains a near-dominating vertex; deleting it leaves an
$F$-saturated graph on its neighborhood, so the deficiency decomposes additively. Cameron and
Puleo make this rigorous for $t = 1, 2$.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Algorithm A (brute-force $\sat$ evaluator).} Enumerate graphs on $n$ vertices, filter
to $H$-saturated ones by definition, return the minimum edge count; $O(2^{\binom n2})$ graphs
with a subgraph-isomorphism test per nonedge.

\textbf{Algorithm B (cone constructor/checker).} Build $K_1 \vee H$ by adding an all-ones apex
row/column and verify $e(K_1 \vee H) = |V(H)| + e(H)$; $O(m^2)$.

\textbf{Algorithm C (recurrence verifier).} For given $t, q$ and a range of $n$, compute both
sides of $(\star)$ using Algorithm~A and compare.

\section{Applications}
Saturated graphs model networks built as cheaply as possible while one edge from a forbidden
configuration; $(\star)$ shows hub-and-matching optima are hierarchical. Exact values from
$(\star)$ benchmark extremal solvers, and the ratio $\sat/\ex \to 0$ with its rate quantifies
pattern rigidity.

\section{Discussion and future work}
We made rigorous the foundational triad (Theorems~\ref{thm:exist},~\ref{thm:satex},
~\ref{thm:cone}) and stated $(\star)$ with the open inequality $(\dagger)$. The natural route
to the general case is induction on the matching size $t$: peel one matching edge and propagate
the recurrence. A companion bridge to Tur\'an's theorem is $\sat(n, K_{r+1}) \le e(T(n,r))$.
The headline open problem is to prove $(\dagger)$, hence $(\star)$, for all $t \ge 1$.

